  ('cvh-019-02', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 19, 2, 'LESSON III.', 'Most Chinese nouns signifying objects take before them a syllable or word usually termed by foreigners a classifier* or numerative. This is, to a certain extent, generic; i.e. a similar word applies to a large number of nouns having some analogy to each other. Thus the word CHI, meaning a branch, is applied to pens, candles, whips, pencils, muskets, and other straight things; e.g. a pen, YAT CHI PAT, 一枝筆; a whip, YAT CHI MA PIN, 一枝馬鞭.

The adjective comes between the classifier and its noun, when following a numeral; thus, a large pen, YAT CHI TAI PAT, 一枝大筆.

The classifiers are distinguished in the vocabularies of these lessons by being placed within brackets.

<table><tr><td>Kuife (table)</td><td>張刀</td><td>(chenng) to</td><td>This</td><td>呢, 呢個.</td><td>ni, ni-ko&#x27;.</td></tr><tr><td>Wine</td><td>酒</td><td>tsau&#x27;</td><td>These</td><td>呢啲</td><td>ni-ti</td></tr><tr><td>Tumbler</td><td>水杯</td><td>shui&#x27;-pui</td><td>That</td><td>响, 响個.</td><td>ko&#x27;, ko&#x27;-ko&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>Plate</td><td>隻碟</td><td>(chek) tip</td><td>Those</td><td>個啲</td><td>ko&#x27;-ti</td></tr><tr><td>Fork</td><td>枝叉</td><td>(chi) ch&#x27;a</td><td>No</td><td>唔係</td><td>&#x27;m hai&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>Spoon</td><td>隻匙羹</td><td>(chek) shi-kang</td><td>Not, neg. part.</td><td>唔, 冇, 不</td><td>&#x27;m, mo&#x27;, pat</td></tr></table>

* Note.—Although I have here adopted the conventional term applied by foreigners to a large number of words which are constantly used in combination with nouns, it may be well to point out that the designation thus used is to a great extent a misnomer. There are, strictly speaking, but eight or nine "classifiers," i.e. words which have no analogous terms in our own language. It is obviously absurd to denominate such words as pair, flock, frame, lump, string, suit, cluster, &c. "classifiers," they being in reality either simple nouns or nouns of multitude. The term, being however convenient, is adhered to, with this protest against possible misconception.', 'MostChinesenounssignifyingobjectstakebeforethemasyllableorwordusuallytermedbyforeignersaclassifier*ornumerative.Thisis,toacertainextent,generic;i.e.asimilarwordappliestoalargenumberofnounshavingsomeanalogytoeachother.ThusthewordCHI,meaningabranch,isappliedtopens,candles,whips,pencils,muskets,andotherstraightthings;e.g.apen,YATCHIPAT,一枝筆;awhip,YATCHIMAPIN,一枝馬鞭.Theadjectivecomesbetweentheclassifieranditsnoun,whenfollowinganumeral;thus,alargepen,YATCHITAIPAT,一枝大筆.Theclassifiersaredistinguishedinthevocabulariesoftheselessonsbybeingplacedwithinbrackets.<table><tr><td>Kuife(table)</td><td>張刀</td><td>(chenng)to</td><td>This</td><td>呢,呢個.</td><td>ni,ni-ko&#x27;.</td></tr><tr><td>Wine</td><td>酒</td><td>tsau&#x27;</td><td>These</td><td>呢啲</td><td>ni-ti</td></tr><tr><td>Tumbler</td><td>水杯</td><td>shui&#x27;-pui</td><td>That</td><td>响,响個.</td><td>ko&#x27;,ko&#x27;-ko&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>Plate</td><td>隻碟</td><td>(chek)tip</td><td>Those</td><td>個啲</td><td>ko&#x27;-ti</td></tr><tr><td>Fork</td><td>枝叉</td><td>(chi)ch&#x27;a</td><td>No</td><td>唔係</td><td>&#x27;mhai&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>Spoon</td><td>隻匙羹</td><td>(chek)shi-kang</td><td>Not,neg.part.</td><td>唔,冇,不</td><td>&#x27;m,mo&#x27;,pat</td></tr></table>*Note.—AlthoughIhavehereadoptedtheconventionaltermappliedbyforeignerstoalargenumberofwordswhichareconstantlyusedincombinationwithnouns,itmaybewelltopointoutthatthedesignationthususedistoagreatextentamisnomer.Thereare,strictlyspeaking,buteightornine"classifiers,"i.e.wordswhichhavenoanalogoustermsinourownlanguage.Itisobviouslyabsurdtodenominatesuchwordsaspair,flock,frame,lump,string,suit,cluster,&c."classifiers,"theybeinginrealityeithersimplenounsornounsofmultitude.Theterm,beinghoweverconvenient,isadheredto,withthisprotestagainstpossiblemisconception.', 'verified'),
  ('cvh-020-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 20, 1, NULL, 'Rice (used to express food generally) 飯 fan* Yes 係 hai''

Wine-glass 酒杯 tsau''-pui Large 大 tai''

Is, it is, are 係 hai'' Small 細 sai''

NI-ti 呢啲 ko-ti 個啲, though properly plural forms, are used for this and that before words signifying fluids, or matter consisting of small particles such as sand, dust, flour, &c., and occasionally before other words.

Yes and no are expressed by the verb to be, joined, in the case of no, to a negative particle; thus, yes is expressed by it is, and no by it is not.

Adjectives precede the substantives they qualify, as in English.

1 Bring a plate 擰隻碟來 ning chek tip loi

2 Give me the knife 俾張刀我 pi cheung to ngo

3 Have you the fork (and) spoon? 你有枝叉,有匙羹呀 ni yau chi ch''a, yau shi-kang a?

4 I have the plate (and) the fork 我有隻碟, 有枝叉 ngo yau chek tip, yau chi ch''a

5 (It) is wine 係酒 hai tsau

6 I want a wine-glass 我要酒杯 ngo iu tsau-pui

7 This (is) large 呢個大 ni ko tai

8 This is large tea-cup 呢個係大茶杯 ni ko hai tai ch''a-pui

9 Get the sugar spoon 擰白糖羹來 ning pak-t''ong kang loi

10 Have you (any) wine? 你有酒冇 ni yau tsau mo?

11 I have wine, I (have) no tumbler 我有酒, 我有水杯 ngo yau tsau, ngo mo shui-pui

12 This is a tumbler, that is a 呢個係水杯, 個啲 ni ko hai shui-pui, ko ti hai wine-glass 係酒杯 tsau-pui

13 Bring tea, (and) a spoon 擰茶拈匙羹來 ning ch''a, nim shi-kang loi

14 Give me (some) water 俾水我 pi shui ngo

15 Have you a wine-glass? 你有酒杯冇 ni yau tsau-pui mo?

16 I (have) no wine-glass, (I) have 我有酒杯, 有茶杯 ngo mo tsau-pui, yau ch''a-pui a tea-cup

17 Is that tea?

响啲係茶咩 ko ti hai ch''a me?

18 Yes, (it) is tea

係; 係茶 hai; hai ch''a

