  ('cvh-054-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 54, 1, 'AUXILIARY VERBS.', 'As in all other languages, certain verbs in frequent use are auxiliary to others.

Of these the most useful in Chinese (in which the strictly auxiliary uses of "to have" and "to be" are not found,) are the following:—TAK得 to be able, can; CHEUK着 implying action; SHAI便 to bid; LING令 to make or cause; and PI俾 to give, cause, make, or let.

PIT 必 must and sü 須 necessary (with other expressions of which they are components) meaning must, ought to, may be deemed auxiliaries; as may also several others which will be given in the lessons immediately following.

肯 HANG, to be willing, to assent to, is used to express the future tense when intention is implied. (Vide sentence 4, &c.)

1 Send that man for a receipt 使响個人去攞收單 shai ko ko yan hü lo shau-tan

2 Tell him he must pay the rent 話佢知是必要納租銀 wa k''ü chi shi pit iu nap tso-ngăn

3 He can''t give a receipt 佢唔俾得收單 k''ü''m pi täk shau-tan

4 He will not give a receipt yet 佢唔肯現時開收單 k''u''m hang in shi hoi shau-tan

5 Has he ever set a sail in a boat? 佢有在過三板扯𢃇唔曾 k''ü yau tsoi kwo sam-pan ch''é li'' ''m ts''ang?

6 He did so formerly 佢先時做過 k''ü sin-shi tso kwo

7 He is going to row now 佢現時想去棹槳 k''u in-shi seung hü chau tseung

8 I never signed my name 我唔曾簽過名 ngo ''m ts''ang ts''im kwo meng

9 She often sews clothes 佢好多回聯衣服咯 k''u ho to ui lün i-fuk lok

10 Sometimes he pays and sometimes he does''nt 佢有時俾銀有時唔俾 k''u yau shi pi ngün, yau shi ''m pi

11 Did he set sail then? 佢係個陣時開身唔係 k''u hai ko chan shi hoi shan ''m hai

12 When he gave the receipt he had no money 佢開收單時有銀 k''u hoi shau-tan shi mo ngan

13 We do not often sleep there 我哋冇幾多回喺個處瞓 ngo ti mo ki to ui hai ko-ch''ü fān

14 I observe that the flowers smell very well 我聞見响啲花好香 ngo măn kin ko-ti fa ho heung

