Several words in addition to those already given are, by some grammarians, described as auxiliary verbs, their only claim to that title, however, being the frequency with which they occur in compound verbal phrases. Such are CHUNG-I 中意 to like, KOM 敢 to dare, &c.

To have, YAU 有, is frequently used (much as in German) for to be; e.g. to be ill is YAU PENG 有病 "to have illness." (comp. hunger, durst haben, &c.)

