  ('cvh-083-02', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 83, 2, 'LESSON XXIX.', 'The accusative or objective case (as we term it) generally follows the verb in simple phrases, but in long sentences occasionally precedes it.

What we should in other languages term the ablative is rendered either by the prepositions tsz自, yau由, &c.; by using words, not properly prepositions, in that sense; or by transposing the sentence; e.g.

He comes from Hongkong 佢喺香港來 k''ü'' hai Heung-kong'' loi

He comes by ship 佢搭船來 k''ü'' tap shün loi

Killed by a sword 用刀殺人 yung'' to shat yan (lit. us- [ing]

a sword, killed one)

A sheet of paper 一張紙 yat cheung chi'' To seal 封住 fung chü''

A sedan chair 一頂轎 yat ting'' kiu'' To connect 連住 lin chü''

To envelop (wrap up) 包起 pau hi'' To guard 守住 shau'' chü''

To draw to pull up 揪起 ts''au hi'' To bar 關住 kwan chü''

The heavens, sky 天 t''in A village 條村 t''iu ts''ün

Diminutives are expressed by siu 小 (already given) or sai 細 small, preceding the noun, and tsai 仔 following it; e.g., a small house, SIU KAN UK; or YAT KAN UK-TSAI, 小間屋或一間屋仔; a small knife, TO-TSAI 刀仔.

CLASSIFIERS.

