40 Tell him to mix those things 叫佢子細的攪勻响 kiu k''ü tsz-sai tik kau-wän ko-together carefully 啲野 ti ye', '39Notabitofit;hewon''t一的都唔係;佢唔yattikto''mhai;k''ü''mloikecome來嘅咯lok40Tellhimtomixthosethings叫佢子細的攪勻响kiuk''ütsz-saitikkau-wänko-togethercarefully啲野tiye', 'verified'),
  ('cvh-098-02', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 98, 2, 'LESSON XXXIII.', '<table border="1"><tr><td>Good</td><td>好</td><td>ho&#x27;</td><td>Bad</td><td>惡</td><td>ok</td></tr><tr><td>Better</td><td>更好</td><td>kǎng&#x27; ho&#x27;</td><td>Worse</td><td>更惡</td><td>kǎng&#x27; ok</td></tr><tr><td>Best</td><td>至好</td><td>chi&#x27; ho&#x27;</td><td>Worst</td><td>至惡</td><td>chi&#x27; ok</td></tr><tr><td>Short</td><td>短</td><td>tün&#x27;</td><td>White</td><td>白</td><td>pak</td></tr><tr><td>shorter</td><td>更短</td><td>kǎng&#x27; tün&#x27;</td><td>Whiter</td><td>更白</td><td>kǎng&#x27; pak</td></tr><tr><td>Shortest</td><td>至短</td><td>chi&#x27; tün&#x27;</td><td>Whitest</td><td>至白</td><td>chi&#x27; pak</td></tr><tr><td>Much</td><td>多</td><td>to</td><td>Little</td><td>少</td><td>siu&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>More</td><td>重多</td><td>chung&#x27; to</td><td>Less</td><td>更少</td><td>kǎng&#x27; siu&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>Most</td><td>至多</td><td>chi&#x27; to</td><td>Least</td><td>至少</td><td>chi&#x27; siu&#x27;</td></tr></table>

To form the superlative ke 嘅 is sometimes used after the adjective, which takes chi 至 or tai yat 大一, before it; e.g. The most distant mountain chi ün ke shan 至遠嘅山.

Other ways of forming the superlative are:—

(a) By placing TAK TSAI 得嚌 KWO T''AU 過頭 or PAT SHING 不勝 after the adjective.

(b) By placing KIK 極 before, and KE 嘅 after the adjective.

SHAP-FAN 十分 and SHEUNG 上 are also used to express very, the highest.

CLASSIFIERS (continued)

25. Ka''架 a wooden frame, is applied to stands, fire-engines, &c.

26. To'' 朵 hanging branches, is applied to flowers, fire, flames, etc.

27. P''in 片 a slip or fragment, is applied to slices, slips, bits, etc. of various articles.

28. Tsek 蓆 a mat, is applied to spots of ground, feasts, conversations, &c. (Seldom used, but included in most lists of classifiers.)', '<tableborder="1"><tr><td>Good</td><td>好</td><td>ho&#x27;</td><td>Bad</td><td>惡</td><td>ok</td></tr><tr><td>Better</td><td>更好</td><td>kǎng&#x27;ho&#x27;</td><td>Worse</td><td>更惡</td><td>kǎng&#x27;ok</td></tr><tr><td>Best</td><td>至好</td><td>chi&#x27;ho&#x27;</td><td>Worst</td><td>至惡</td><td>chi&#x27;ok</td></tr><tr><td>Short</td><td>短</td><td>tün&#x27;</td><td>White</td><td>白</td><td>pak</td></tr><tr><td>shorter</td><td>更短</td><td>kǎng&#x27;tün&#x27;</td><td>Whiter</td><td>更白</td><td>kǎng&#x27;pak</td></tr><tr><td>Shortest</td><td>至短</td><td>chi&#x27;tün&#x27;</td><td>Whitest</td><td>至白</td><td>chi&#x27;pak</td></tr><tr><td>Much</td><td>多</td><td>to</td><td>Little</td><td>少</td><td>siu&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>More</td><td>重多</td><td>chung&#x27;to</td><td>Less</td><td>更少</td><td>kǎng&#x27;siu&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>Most</td><td>至多</td><td>chi&#x27;to</td><td>Least</td><td>至少</td><td>chi&#x27;siu&#x27;</td></tr></table>Toformthesuperlativeke嘅issometimesusedaftertheadjective,whichtakeschi至ortaiyat大一,beforeit;e.g.Themostdistantmountainchiünkeshan至遠嘅山.Otherwaysofformingthesuperlativeare:—(a)ByplacingTAKTSAI得嚌KWOT''AU過頭orPATSHING不勝aftertheadjective.(b)ByplacingKIK極before,andKE嘅aftertheadjective.SHAP-FAN十分andSHEUNG上arealsousedtoexpressvery,thehighest.CLASSIFIERS(continued)25.Ka''架awoodenframe,isappliedtostands,fire-engines,&c.26.To''朵hangingbranches,isappliedtoflowers,fire,flames,etc.27.P''in片asliporfragment,isappliedtoslices,slips,bits,etc.ofvariousarticles.28.Tsek蓆amat,isappliedtospotsofground,feasts,conversations,&c.(Seldomused,butincludedinmostlistsofclassifiers.)', 'verified'),
  ('cvh-099-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 99, 1, NULL, '29. Pau 包 a bundle, is applied to bales, bundles, or packages of goods.

