  ('cvh-109-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 109, 1, NULL, '<table border="1"><tr><td>28 No, he has ceased to do anything</td><td>冇，佢歇手唔做事幹咯</td><td>mo, k&#x27;ü hit shau &#x27;m tso sze-kon lok</td></tr><tr><td>29 Tell the wet nurse to carry that baby</td><td>呌個奶媽抱嗰個孩子</td><td>kiu ko nai-ma p&#x27;e ko-ko hoi-tsz</td></tr><tr><td>30 Some ladies came with him</td><td>有幾個同師奶佢來</td><td>yau ki ko sze-nai t&#x27;ung k&#x27;ü loi</td></tr><tr><td>31 How many gentlemen are coming to-night?</td><td>今晚有幾多個人客來呢</td><td>kām-man yau ki to ko yan-hak lei ni?</td></tr><tr><td>32 I don&#x27;t know; ask the boy (servant)</td><td>我唔知問事仔喇</td><td>ngo &#x27;m chi; mǎn sze-tsai la</td></tr><tr><td>33 It is wrong to despise others</td><td>輕忽人係唔着嘅</td><td>hing-fat yan hai &#x27;m chenk ke</td></tr><tr><td>34 He is very rich man, people say</td><td>人哋話，佢係一個十分財主嘅人</td><td>yan-ti wa k&#x27;ü hai yat ko shap-fan ts&#x27;oi-chü ke yan</td></tr><tr><td>35 He does nothing but play the guitar</td><td>佢乜都唔做，獨係彈琵琶</td><td>k&#x27;ü măt to &#x27;m tso, tuk hai t&#x27;an p&#x27;i-p&#x27;a</td></tr><tr><td>36 I thought he played the fiddle</td><td>我估佢彈三絃</td><td>ngo ku k&#x27;ü t&#x27;an sam-in</td></tr><tr><td>37 You are mistaken; he does neither (does not play either)</td><td>你估錯咯，佢兩樣都唔彈</td><td>ni ku ts&#x27;oh lok; k&#x27;ü leung yeung to &#x27;m t&#x27;an</td></tr><tr><td>38 Seud for an engraver to do that</td><td>呌個彫匠做嗰啲喇</td><td>kiu ko tiu-tseung tso ko-ti la</td></tr><tr><td>39 Is the fruit well flavoured?</td><td>嗰的生菓好味道唔呢</td><td>ko-ti shāng-kwo ho mi-to &#x27;m ni?</td></tr><tr><td>40 No, it is not at all well flavoured</td><td>唔係，唔係好味道得啫</td><td>&#x27;m hai, &#x27;m hai ho mi-to tāk che</td></tr></table>', '<tableborder="1"><tr><td>28No,hehasceasedtodoanything</td><td>冇，佢歇手唔做事幹咯</td><td>mo,k&#x27;ühitshau&#x27;mtsosze-konlok</td></tr><tr><td>29Tellthewetnursetocarrythatbaby</td><td>呌個奶媽抱嗰個孩子</td><td>kiukonai-map&#x27;eko-kohoi-tsz</td></tr><tr><td>30Someladiescamewithhim</td><td>有幾個同師奶佢來</td><td>yaukikosze-nait&#x27;ungk&#x27;üloi</td></tr><tr><td>31Howmanygentlemenarecomingto-night?</td><td>今晚有幾多個人客來呢</td><td>kām-manyaukitokoyan-hakleini?</td></tr><tr><td>32Idon&#x27;tknow;asktheboy(servant)</td><td>我唔知問事仔喇</td><td>ngo&#x27;mchi;mǎnsze-tsaila</td></tr><tr><td>33Itiswrongtodespiseothers</td><td>輕忽人係唔着嘅</td><td>hing-fatyanhai&#x27;mchenkke</td></tr><tr><td>34Heisveryrichman,peoplesay</td><td>人哋話，佢係一個十分財主嘅人</td><td>yan-tiwak&#x27;ühaiyatkoshap-fants&#x27;oi-chükeyan</td></tr><tr><td>35Hedoesnothingbutplaytheguitar</td><td>佢乜都唔做，獨係彈琵琶</td><td>k&#x27;ümătto&#x27;mtso,tukhait&#x27;anp&#x27;i-p&#x27;a</td></tr><tr><td>36Ithoughtheplayedthefiddle</td><td>我估佢彈三絃</td><td>ngokuk&#x27;üt&#x27;ansam-in</td></tr><tr><td>37Youaremistaken;hedoesneither(doesnotplayeither)</td><td>你估錯咯，佢兩樣都唔彈</td><td>nikuts&#x27;ohlok;k&#x27;üleungyeungto&#x27;mt&#x27;an</td></tr><tr><td>38Seudforanengravertodothat</td><td>呌個彫匠做嗰啲喇</td><td>kiukotiu-tseungtsoko-tila</td></tr><tr><td>39Isthefruitwellflavoured?</td><td>嗰的生菓好味道唔呢</td><td>ko-tishāng-kwohomi-to&#x27;mni?</td></tr><tr><td>40No,itisnotatallwellflavoured</td><td>唔係，唔係好味道得啫</td><td>&#x27;mhai,&#x27;mhaihomi-totākche</td></tr></table>', 'needs_review'),
  ('cvh-109-02', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 109, 2, 'LESSON XXXVI.', 'Following out the idea explained in the previous lesson, self depreciatory phrases are constantly used by the Chinese in referring to their own relations, designations and possessions. Thus my wife is expressed by TSIN FONG 賤房 "the mean one within the house;" TUK FU 獨婦 "the lonely woman;" SHAN TS''AI 山妻 "(my) rustic (lit. mountain) uife."', 'Followingouttheideaexplainedinthepreviouslesson,selfdepreciatoryphrasesareconstantlyusedbytheChineseinreferringtotheirownrelations,designationsandpossessions.ThusmywifeisexpressedbyTSINFONG賤房"themeanonewithinthehouse;"TUKFU獨婦"thelonelywoman;"SHANTS''AI山妻"(my)rustic(lit.mountain)uife."', 'needs_review'),
  ('cvh-110-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 110, 1, NULL, '<table><tr><td>My house</td><td>敝屋</td><td>pai`uk</td><td>(vile dwelling)</td></tr><tr><td>My country</td><td>敝國</td><td>pai`kwok</td><td>(vile country)</td></tr><tr><td>My surname</td><td>賤姓</td><td>tsin`sing&#x27;</td><td>(mean surname)</td></tr><tr><td>Son</td><td>小兒</td><td>siu&#x27;-i</td><td>(little child)</td></tr></table>

It is of course on ceremonious occasions only that such words as are given above are much used. The speaker at such times is expected, on the other hand, to use complimentary phrases when alluding to the relations, &c. of the person addressed. Of these examples will be found in the next lesson.

To hold a horse 牽住匹馬 hin chü'' p''at ma'' To be safe 穩咯 wăn'' lok

To wash one''s hands 洗手 sai'' shau'' To mistake 記錯 ki''tsoh''

To guess, think, believe 估 ku'' (remember wrongly)', '<table><tr><td>Myhouse</td><td>敝屋</td><td>pai`uk</td><td>(viledwelling)</td></tr><tr><td>Mycountry</td><td>敝國</td><td>pai`kwok</td><td>(vilecountry)</td></tr><tr><td>Mysurname</td><td>賤姓</td><td>tsin`sing&#x27;</td><td>(meansurname)</td></tr><tr><td>Son</td><td>小兒</td><td>siu&#x27;-i</td><td>(littlechild)</td></tr></table>Itisofcourseonceremoniousoccasionsonlythatsuchwordsasaregivenabovearemuchused.Thespeakeratsuchtimesisexpected,ontheotherhand,tousecomplimentaryphraseswhenalludingtotherelations,&c.ofthepersonaddressed.Oftheseexampleswillbefoundinthenextlesson.Toholdahorse牽住匹馬hinchü''p''atma''Tobesafe穩咯wăn''lokTowashone''shands洗手sai''shau''Tomistake記錯ki''tsoh''Toguess,think,believe估ku''(rememberwrongly)', 'needs_review'),
  ('cvh-110-02', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 110, 2, 'CLASSIFIERS (continued.)', '31. Fung封 to seal, is applied to letters and despatches.

