The relative pronoun is in Cantonese expressed by the insertion of the particle KE 嘅 after the relative phrase; e.g., those who study this are good men, is, literally, "study this-KE (嘅) are good men," HOK NI-TI KE HAI HO YAN 學呢啲嘅係好人. The house I was born in was built long since, NGO CH''UT SHAI KO KAN UK HAI HI HIU HO KAU KE LOK 我出世嗰間屋係起嘵好久嘅咯. The men I require are these, NGO SHAI KO-TI YAN HAI NI-TI KE LOK 我使嗰啲人係呢啲嘅咯.

The reflective pronouns self, selves, are expressed by tsz-ki自己,and tsz- KA自家.

Himself 佢自己 k''ü''-tsz`-ki'' Each other, one對, ti''

Do. 自家 tsz''-ka another每mui''

Self, oneself 自己 tsz''-ki'' Mutual相seung

CLASSIFIERS (continued.)

37. Ha''吓 a time, a while, is applied to blows with a hand or stick; (strike one blow:

TA YAT HA SHAU 打一吓手.)

38. Ch''ün 串 to connect, ,,

39. Shing'' 乘 to ride, ,

things strung together such as beads, &c. to chairs (sedans) and carriages (TING頂 is also used for sedan chairs.)

40. T''uit 脆 a pattern, ,, suits of clothes.

41. Ku'' 股 a share, proportion, ,, heads of an essay, shares in business.

42. Kuk 局 a chessboard square, ,, games of chess and chess boards.

It must be understood by the student that the meanings placed against the classifiers are in many cases but some of several significations attached to the characters, which are not only classifiers but independent words.

