  ('cvh-132-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 132, 1, 'INDEFINITE PRONOUNS.', 'The indefinite pronouns are as follow:—

<table><tr><td>Everybody, everyone</td><td>各人</td><td>kok yan</td><td rowspan="2">One and all</td><td colspan="2">一齊(一總) yat ts''ai (or yas)</td></tr><tr><td>Do.</td><td>每人</td><td>mui'' yan</td><td colspan="2">咁多人 tsung'' kom'' to yan</td></tr><tr><td>Both, a pair of</td><td>兩個</td><td>leung'' ko''</td><td rowspan="2">One (as an indefinite pronoun)</td><td rowspan="2" colspan="2">yat</td></tr><tr><td>Do.</td><td>雙</td><td>sheung</td></tr><tr><td>One another</td><td>各人</td><td>kok yan</td><td>Everything</td><td>各件</td><td>kok kin''</td></tr><tr><td>Do.</td><td>大家</td><td>tai'' ku</td><td>Nothing</td><td>無野</td><td>mo ye''</td></tr><tr><td>Very many, most</td><td>大多</td><td>tai'' to</td><td>Such</td><td>噉樣</td><td>kom'' yeung''</td></tr><tr><td>Certain men</td><td>或人</td><td>wak yan</td><td>Either</td><td>或</td><td>wak</td></tr><tr><td>Somebody</td><td>有人</td><td>yau'' yan</td><td>Do. (classical)</td><td>是但</td><td>shi'' tan''</td></tr><tr><td>Several (indefinite)</td><td>幾個</td><td>ki'' ko''</td><td colspan="3">“A certain one” can</td></tr><tr><td>Few (do.)</td><td>幾個</td><td>,,</td><td colspan="2">be expressed</td><td rowspan="4">有人 yau'' yan</td></tr><tr><td>None, not any</td><td>無</td><td>mo</td><td colspan="2">verbally; e.g. A</td></tr><tr><td>Each, every</td><td>每</td><td>mui''</td><td colspan="2">certain man</td></tr><tr><td>Little</td><td>少</td><td>shin''</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>

The word "people" is expressed by YAN人 (one, certain persons). Most of the above are used as in English, many being interchangeable. Thus several and few are at times used indifferently to convey the idea of an indefinite number of people.', 'Theindefinitepronounsareasfollow:—<table><tr><td>Everybody,everyone</td><td>各人</td><td>kokyan</td><tdrowspan="2">Oneandall</td><tdcolspan="2">一齊(一總)yatts''ai(oryas)</td></tr><tr><td>Do.</td><td>每人</td><td>mui''yan</td><tdcolspan="2">咁多人tsung''kom''toyan</td></tr><tr><td>Both,apairof</td><td>兩個</td><td>leung''ko''</td><tdrowspan="2">One(asanindefinitepronoun)</td><tdrowspan="2"colspan="2">yat</td></tr><tr><td>Do.</td><td>雙</td><td>sheung</td></tr><tr><td>Oneanother</td><td>各人</td><td>kokyan</td><td>Everything</td><td>各件</td><td>kokkin''</td></tr><tr><td>Do.</td><td>大家</td><td>tai''ku</td><td>Nothing</td><td>無野</td><td>moye''</td></tr><tr><td>Verymany,most</td><td>大多</td><td>tai''to</td><td>Such</td><td>噉樣</td><td>kom''yeung''</td></tr><tr><td>Certainmen</td><td>或人</td><td>wakyan</td><td>Either</td><td>或</td><td>wak</td></tr><tr><td>Somebody</td><td>有人</td><td>yau''yan</td><td>Do.(classical)</td><td>是但</td><td>shi''tan''</td></tr><tr><td>Several(indefinite)</td><td>幾個</td><td>ki''ko''</td><tdcolspan="3">“Acertainone”can</td></tr><tr><td>Few(do.)</td><td>幾個</td><td>,,</td><tdcolspan="2">beexpressed</td><tdrowspan="4">有人yau''yan</td></tr><tr><td>None,notany</td><td>無</td><td>mo</td><tdcolspan="2">verbally;e.g.A</td></tr><tr><td>Each,every</td><td>每</td><td>mui''</td><tdcolspan="2">certainman</td></tr><tr><td>Little</td><td>少</td><td>shin''</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>Theword"people"isexpressedbyYAN人(one,certainpersons).MostoftheaboveareusedasinEnglish,manybeinginterchangeable.Thusseveralandfewareattimesusedindifferentlytoconveytheideaofanindefinitenumberofpeople.', 'verified'),
  ('cvh-132-02', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 132, 2, 'CLASSIFIERS (continued)', '49. Fong 方 a square, is applied to squares of ink, inkstones, junkets of beef, mutton, pork, &c.

