  ('cvh-151-03', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 151, 3, 'PASSIVE VERBS.', 'The passive voice of Chinese verbs may be formed by adding certain particles to the root or infinitive; but most sentences which take a passive form in English are preferentially rendered in Chinese by the active form of the verb, and a corresponding change in the sentence. Thus "I have been beaten by him" would be rendered by "He has beaten me" 佢打親我 K''Ü TA TS''ĂN NGO. The tendency of Chinese colloquial is towards simplification, and this is aided by the habit of making sentences as short as possible, thus to a great extent dispensing with passive formations, relatives, etc. As the student, however, should be acquainted with the passive voice, an example is given hereunder, the formative particles being in brackets. These formations are not, strictly speaking, colloquial, but are perfectly well understood, and are at times used, by educated Cantonese.

I am beaten 我 (被打) 親 ngo'' (pi'' ta'') ts''ăn

I am hated 我 (被見) 惡 ngo'' (pi'' kin'') u''

I was beaten 我 (曾被) 打親 ngo'' (ts''ăng pi'') ta'' ts''ăn

I was cheated 我 (曾被) 騙 ngo'' (ts''ăng pi'') p''in''', 'ThepassivevoiceofChineseverbsmaybeformedbyaddingcertainparticlestotherootorinfinitive;butmostsentenceswhichtakeapassiveforminEnglisharepreferentiallyrenderedinChinesebytheactiveformoftheverb,andacorrespondingchangeinthesentence.Thus"Ihavebeenbeatenbyhim"wouldberenderedby"Hehasbeatenme"佢打親我K''ÜTATS''ĂNNGO.ThetendencyofChinesecolloquialistowardssimplification,andthisisaidedbythehabitofmakingsentencesasshortaspossible,thustoagreatextentdispensingwithpassiveformations,relatives,etc.Asthestudent,however,shouldbeacquaintedwiththepassivevoice,anexampleisgivenhereunder,theformativeparticlesbeinginbrackets.Theseformationsarenot,strictlyspeaking,colloquial,butareperfectlywellunderstood,andareattimesused,byeducatedCantonese.Iambeaten我(被打)親ngo''(pi''ta'')ts''ănIamhated我(被見)惡ngo''(pi''kin'')u''Iwasbeaten我(曾被)打親ngo''(ts''ăngpi'')ta''ts''ănIwascheated我(曾被)騙ngo''(ts''ăngpi'')p''in''', 'needs_review'),
  ('cvh-152-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 152, 1, NULL, '<table><tr><td>I may be loved</td><td>我(或者見)愛</td><td>ngo'' (wak che'' kin'') oi</td></tr><tr><td>He may be hated</td><td>佢(或者見)惡</td><td>k''ü'' (wak che'' kin'') u''</td></tr><tr><td>I might be taught</td><td>我(或者可蒙)教</td><td>ngo'' (wak che'' ho'' mung) kau''</td></tr><tr><td>He might be pitied</td><td>佢(或者可蒙見)憐</td><td>k''ü'' (wak che'' ho'' mung) liu</td></tr></table>

