his) welfare. He said:—''Still longer still-more (I) feel not at-peace;'' also (he) took yesterday those words (and) spoke to them to-hear. They at-all (would) not pay-attention-to (or heed) him, (the) intention (being) in-order-to not to-be courteous to him, perhaps (he) might (get) well again: so then there-were times (they) jeered-at him, or angrily scolded him, or at-all not attended-to him. Therefore he himself entered (and) went-into (his) room midst: on-one hand (he) was-sad (on-account-of) his own sins (and) evil; on-the-other hand (he) pitied (his) family people in-darkness (and) not awakened; (and) on-behalf-of them (he) prayed. Again every-now-and-then, only himself out went to-the-fields midst, either to-look-at (his) book, or to-pray: in-one connected (series of) several days (he) even was (like) so.', 'k''aitsok₂-yat₂ko''-tishüt₀-wa²,kongkwo''k''ou-tei²t''eng†.K''öü-téi²tsungmts''oik''öü,yi''-szyi-wai²mpeits''ing-leitoi²k''öü,wak²cheho-yihòfau:komtsau²yaushich''i-siúk''öü,wak₂no²-ma²k''öü,wak₂tsungmleik''öü.Sho-yik''öütsz²-kéiyap₂höufongchung:yattsakpai''-aitsz²-kéitsou²ok;yattsakho-línkáyanch''i-maipatsing;t''aík''öü-téi²k''éi''tó.Yau²mui''-muituk₂tsz²-kéi(yatko'')ch''uthöu''t''inchung,wak''t''aïshu,wak₂k''ei''tó:yatlinkéiyat₂tohai²kóm.his)welfare.Hesaid:—''Stilllongerstill-more(I)feelnotat-peace;''also(he)tookyesterdaythosewords(and)spoketothemto-hear.Theyat-all(would)notpay-attention-to(orheed)him,(the)intention(being)in-order-tonotto-becourteoustohim,perhaps(he)might(get)wellagain:sothenthere-weretimes(they)jeered-athim,orangrilyscoldedhim,orat-allnotattended-tohim.Thereforehehimselfentered(and)went-into(his)roommidst:on-onehand(he)was-sad(on-account-of)hisownsins(and)evil;on-the-otherhand(he)pitied(his)familypeoplein-darkness(and)notawakened;(and)on-behalf-ofthem(he)prayed.Againevery-now-and-then,onlyhimselfoutwentto-the-fieldsmidst,eitherto-look-at(his)book,orto-pray:in-oneconnected(seriesof)severaldays(he)evenwas(like)so.', 'needs_review'),
  ('rcc-140-01', 'readings-in-cantonese-colloquial', 'readings-in-cantonese-colloquial', '1894', 140, 1, 'LESSON, XXVII.', 'But now, in this Valley of Humiliation, poor Christian was hard put to it; for he had gone but a little way before he espied a foul fiend coming over the field to meet him: his name is Apollyon. Then did Christian begin to be afraid, and to cast in his mind whether to go back or to stand his ground. But he considered again that he had no armour for his back; and therefore thought that to turn the back to him might give him the greater advantage with ease to pierce him with his darts: therefore he resolved to venture and stand his ground; for, thought he, had I no more in mine eye than the saving of my life, it would be the best way to stand.

So he went on, and Apollyon met him. Now the monster was hideous to be held: he was clothed with scales, like a fish (and they are his pride); he had wings like a dragon, feet like a bear, and out of his belly came fire and smoke; and his mouth was as the mouth of a lion. When he was come up to Christian, he beheld

基督徒在居謙谷處，佢嘅情形極苦，因爲行冇幾遠，忽然望見魔鬼，從田野走嚟，佢嘅名叫做亞波淪，個時基督徒心中好驚慌，自己商量話，走翻轉頭好吖，抑或企倒處等佢好呢。又想吓話，我前便有甲着嗻，後便冇得着，倘若走翻轉頭，背便更容易受傷喇，但係想保全生命，當要扎硬企處，唔翻轉頭，噉就立意向前，一路行去。

